$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 11/Signal verification

::EPI-W11-027::Why is a sudden rise in reports not automatically a disease outbreak? {
=Reporting, diagnostics, duplicates or population size may have changed#Correct.
~All surveillance signals are false#Review surveillance purpose and outbreak-investigation logic.
~Outbreaks require randomisation#Review surveillance purpose and outbreak-investigation logic.
~Rates can never rise quickly#Review surveillance purpose and outbreak-investigation logic.
}

::EPI-W11-028::Weekly case reports rose sharply just after a new clinic began reporting. What should be checked before attributing the rise entirely to transmission? {
=Whether the reporting or population change explains some of the increase#Correct.
~Assume the signal proves an outbreak#The observation system itself can change the apparent numerator or denominator.
~Ignore prior baseline data#The observation system itself can change the apparent numerator or denominator.
~Stop using denominators#The observation system itself can change the apparent numerator or denominator.
}

