=Retrospective cohort study#Correct.
~Case-control study only#Review surveillance purpose and outbreak-investigation logic.
~Cross-sectional survey only#Review surveillance purpose and outbreak-investigation logic.
~Randomised trial#Review surveillance purpose and outbreak-investigation logic.
}

::EPI-W11-061::Cases arise from a large population that cannot be fully enumerated. Which design is often practical? {
=Case-control study#Correct.
~Retrospective cohort requiring complete enumeration#Review surveillance purpose and outbreak-investigation logic.
~Randomised trial#Review surveillance purpose and outbreak-investigation logic.
~Ecological time series only#Review surveillance purpose and outbreak-investigation logic.
}

$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 11/Hypotheses and action

::EPI-W11-062::An initial analytic study fails to support the leading outbreak hypothesis. What is the best next step? {
=Revisit descriptive epidemiology, outliers and alternative exposures#Correct.
~Stop the investigation immediately#Review surveillance purpose and outbreak-investigation logic.
~Delete nonsignificant cases#Review surveillance purpose and outbreak-investigation logic.
~Assume the original hypothesis is true#Review surveillance purpose and outbreak-investigation logic.
}

::EPI-W11-063::When can early control measures be reasonable before every uncertainty is resolved? {
=When evidence is sufficiently plausible, delay is harmful and the intervention is proportionate#Correct.
~Only after causal certainty reaches 100%#Public health balances evidence, urgency, harm and reversibility.
~Never during an outbreak#Public health balances evidence, urgency, harm and reversibility.
~Whenever social media demands action#Public health balances evidence, urgency, harm and reversibility.
}

